# Topology: compactness declared, backed by an honest recorded second-order boundary.
ex:ecCompactSpaceDeclared a gmeow:ExampleConformance ;
    gmeow:exampleFile "tests/conformance-fixtures/compact-space-declared.ttl" ;
    gmeow:expectedOutcome gmeow:conforms ;
    gmeow:conformanceRationale "A math:CompactSpace declaring math:isCompact satisfies math:CompactSpaceShape and validates with no findings. Compactness is genuinely second-order (open covers plus finiteness) and so has no first-order law; the fixture instead references the honest recorded boundary math:compactnessBoundary through math:preservesStructure — the boundary is carried in the loss ledger (logic:preservationKind logic:Unsupported), never faked as a first-order logic:Formula." .

ex:ecCompactnessAssumed a gmeow:ExampleConformance ;
    gmeow:exampleFile "tests/counter-examples/compactness-assumed.ttl" ;
    gmeow:expectedOutcome gmeow:violates ;
    gmeow:expectedViolationCode "shacl.MinCountConstraintComponent" ;
    gmeow:conformanceRationale "The counter-example omits math:isCompact on a math:CompactSpace (sh:minCount 1 on math:CompactSpaceShape), so validation MUST emit shacl.MinCountConstraintComponent and NO other finding (math:UndeclaredTopologicalProperty)." .

# Topology: the separation-axiom family is law-backed (T2 the conforming witness).
ex:ecHausdorffSpaceDeclared a gmeow:ExampleConformance ;
    gmeow:exampleFile "tests/conformance-fixtures/hausdorff-space-declared.ttl" ;
    gmeow:expectedOutcome gmeow:conforms ;
    gmeow:conformanceRationale "A math:TopologicalSpace declaring its separation axiom through math:separationAxiom math:hausdorffSeparation and backing it with the first-order law math:hausdorffLaw through math:preservesStructure, with its two points sitting in disjoint open neighbourhoods, validates with no findings. Separation axioms are declared through the math:separationAxiom object property (not a boolean isX) and there is no MinCount gate over them, so the conforming fixture and the well-formed math:hausdorffLaw are the honest witness; the T5 audit verifies each axiom is law-backed." .

